# React Patterns

## Component Structure

Functional components only. Props always destructured in the function signature.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD
function SpotCard(props: SpotCardProps) {
  return <Text>{props.spot.name}</Text>;
}

// ✅ GOOD
export const SpotCard = ({ spot, onPress }: SpotCardProps) => (
  <TouchableOpacity onPress={() => onPress(spot.id)}>
    <Text>{spot.name}</Text>
  </TouchableOpacity>
);
```

## Exports

- Route screens (`app/**/*.tsx`): `export default function ScreenName()`
- Shared components and hooks: named exports (`export const`, `export function`)
- API modules (`lib/supabase/*.ts`): named exports only — no default exports

## Separate Logic from UI

Components render. Hooks compute. API modules fetch.

- A component must not contain data fetching, business logic, or direct Supabase calls
- Extract any non-trivial logic into a custom hook in `hooks/`
- Hooks must not import from `components/`

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — logic inside component
export default function CityScreen() {
  const [spots, setSpots] = useState([]);
  useEffect(() => {
    supabase.from('spots').select('*').then(({ data }) => setSpots(data));
  }, []);
}

// ✅ GOOD — component only renders
export default function CityScreen() {
  const { spots, isLoading } = useSpots(cityId);
  if (isLoading) return <LoadingState />;
  return <SpotList spots={spots} />;
}
```

## File Size

Keep files lean. If a screen file is growing large, extract:
- Repeated JSX blocks → sub-components in the same file or `components/`
- Any `useState` + related logic → a custom hook

## Styles

`StyleSheet.create({})` at the bottom of every component file. Never inline styles except for truly one-off dynamic values (e.g. `{ width: progress * 100 }`). Style keys in camelCase, grouped logically (container first, then children).

```tsx
// ❌ BAD
<View style={{ flex: 1, padding: 16, backgroundColor: '#fff' }}>

// ✅ GOOD
<View style={styles.container}>

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: { flex: 1, padding: 16, backgroundColor: Colors.light.background },
});
```

## State Management

Use the right tool for each type of state — never put state in the wrong layer.

| State type | Tool |
|---|---|
| Server data (spots, cities, tags) | React Query (`useQuery`, `useMutation`) |
| Global client state (auth session, user) | Zustand (`useAuthStore`) |
| Local UI state (modal open, selected tab, input focus) | `useState` |
| Form field values and validation | react-hook-form + Zod |

Do not put server data in Zustand. Do not put UI state in React Query. Do not use `useState` for data that comes from Supabase.

## Business Layer Separation

```
screens (app/)          → render only, call hooks
hooks/                  → React Query, state, derived data
lib/supabase/*.ts       → Supabase API calls, returns typed data
lib/services/*.ts       → third-party APIs (Google Places, Unsplash)
lib/analytics.ts        → PostHog event calls
```

Nothing in `app/` or `components/` imports directly from `utils/supabase.ts`. All data access goes through `lib/supabase/` or `lib/services/`.
